# # dropna fn is used to drop the rows with missing values. By default, it drops the rows where at least one element is missing. You can also specify the axis to drop columns instead of rows.
# # var.dropna(how="any") fn will drop the rows where at least one element is missing, while var.dropna(how="all") will drop the rows where all elements are missing. You can also specify the subset of columns to check for missing values using the subset parameter. For example, var.dropna(subset=["A"]) will drop the rows where column A has missing values.
# # thresh removes the rows which have less than the specified number of non-missing values. For example, var.dropna(thresh=2) will drop the rows that have less than 2 non-missing values.
# var.fillna(0) # fillna fn is used to fill the missing values with a specified value. For example, var.fillna(0) will fill all the missing values with 0. 
# fillna(method="ffill") # method parameter is used to specify the method to fill the missing values. "ffill" will forward fill the missing values, while "bfill" will backward fill the missing values. For example, var.fillna(method="ffill") will fill the missing values with the last non-missing value.